{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the princes offered, etc.</b> It appears that all of them as one wanted to offer on the first day of the dedication of the altar, for they wanted it to be consecrated from all of them together, but HaShem said <b>one prince per day</b>, etc., and He said that throughout all twelve days the dedication of the altar would be reckoned. And therefore after saying <b>one prince</b>, etc., He said a second time <b>for the dedication of the altar</b>, to say as we have written. And here HaShem did not say to Moses ״Take from them״ in the manner that He said above, implying that no authorization was needed for this, for certainly he would accept offerings, and all that is brought to the House of HaShem — gold and silver. Rather, because they came together, HaShem said to him that he should arrange them one prince per day. However, I saw what our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Sifrei, and this is their language: this teaches that just as the princes contributed willingly to the work of the Mishkan, so did they contribute willingly to the dedication of the altar, and Moses did not accept from them until it was said to him from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is stated ״they shall offer their offering for the dedication״, etc. — end quote. Our Rabbis of blessed memory scrutinized the superfluous wording of <b>they shall offer their offering</b>, which there was no need to say, and interpreted it as coming to give permission; from this you learn that he did not accept from them. And we must investigate this: why would Moses refuse to take from their hands the freewill offering to HaShem? And it is possible that he did not want to accept from them until he knew whether HaShem desired that they dedicate the altar, or Moses, or Aaron, and all the more so since he did not see the tribe of Levi among them; and see what we wrote at the beginning of parashat Beha'alotkha. And the same holds true that he was uncertain about each one who came to dedicate the altar with his offering, but apart from this he certainly would have accepted." "<b>And the princes offered, etc.</b> It says a second time <b>and the princes offered</b>, to say that they themselves took the trouble to bring the offering as far as the Mishkan; although they were princes, they did not regard their own honor. Further, it intends that they did not merely bring the offering and hand it to the one who offers it, but rather they themselves offered it before the altar in close proximity to it, to say that these would be offered first with none preceding them. And likewise the verse may intend this in the statement <b>and they offered them before the Mishkan</b>, said regarding the wagons, to say that they dedicated them for the needs of the Mishkan, to carry upon them its boards, etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רז״ל (רבותינו זכרונם לברכה) is rendered as 'our Rabbis of blessed memory'; the same applies to ה׳ throughout, rendered as HaShem per the divine-name rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:38.131681375Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}